    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the Pueblo Revolt, Spanish colonizers founded Mission San José de la Laguna near Encinal around 1699, shaping the area's cultural landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/encinal_new_mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Encinal's hilltop location offered defensive advantages, and a 1930s German‑style Catholic church and a 1926 government school later stood there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/encinal_new_mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Atlantic and Pacific Railroad arrived in the 1880s, bringing jobs and linking Encinal to the emerging Route 66 trade network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
